# SP+ OPERATOR KEY OVERLAY -- NOT PART OF THE RELEASE ISO
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# A kickstart overlay that installs ONE operator SSH public key onto the account
# the installer creates, so Christopher (or a lab technician) can reach a machine
# he installed himself over a real shell. It exists because SP+ deliberately no
# longer ships any key inside the image.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL (D-1, 2026-09-03)
# The image used to carry `config/skel-ssh/authorized_keys` into
# /etc/skel/.ssh/authorized_keys. That meant EVERY machine installed from the ISO
# -- including an advisor's, on an advisor's network, holding client PII --
# trusted the holder of one private key over a listening sshd. That layer and its
# gate are gone; the Containerfile now FAILS the build if anything reappears in
# /etc/skel. The legitimate need behind it (a shell on a machine you own for
# auditing and support) is met here instead, at INSTALL time, per machine, by the
# person doing the install.
#
# THE RULE, AND IT IS NOT NEGOTIABLE
#   * The public release ISO ships NO key anywhere: not in the image, and not in
#     `installer/interactive-defaults.ks`. That release kickstart must contain no
#     `sshkey` line at all -- `grep -c sshkey installer/interactive-defaults.ks`
#     must print 0. It is checked by tests/config-preflight.sh.
#   * This file is an EXAMPLE. It carries a placeholder, never a real key, so it
#     is safe to keep in git. Copy it, fill it in, and keep the filled-in copy out
#     of this repository.
#   * A filled-in copy belongs only on ISOs built for Christopher's own installs
#     and test VMs. If you are unsure whether the ISO you are building is one of
#     those, it is not.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#      Copy this file, fill in BOTH placeholders, and hand it to the installer
#      build -- do not add the include to installer/interactive-defaults.ks:
#          %include /tmp/operator-key.ks
#      or pass it as an additional kickstart to the installer build.
#
# WHY THIS IS A %post AND NOT THE `sshkey` COMMAND -- MEASURED 2026-09-03
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This overlay used to be a single `sshkey --username=... "ssh-ed25519 ..."` line.
# On SP+ THAT DIRECTIVE IS A SILENT NO-OP. It was measured on an RC1c install:
#
#   * Anaconda parsed the key correctly and logged it:
#       Users[2888]: A new ssh key list has been set: [SshKeyData(key='ssh-ed25519
#       AAAA...', username='test')]
#   * It ran AnaTaskThread-SetSshKeysTask-1 and logged "Thread Done".
#     NO ERROR, NO WARNING, EXIT 0, and the install reported success.
#   * /home/test/.ssh DID NOT EXIST on the booted system, and a filesystem-wide
#     search found NO authorized_keys anywhere.
#
# THE CAUSE IS ORDERING, and it is structural to bootc -- not a bug we can wait
# out. Timestamps from that install's journal:
#       19:39:46  bootc deploy writes 01_users.cfg
#       19:39:54  CreateUsersTask, then SetSshKeysTask -- both "Done"
#       19:41     /var/home/test is ACTUALLY CREATED
# On an image-mode system the account is materialised at FIRST BOOT from
# 01_users.cfg. Anaconda writes the key roughly two minutes BEFORE the home
# directory it belongs in exists, and first-boot home creation then supersedes it.
# `/home` is a symlink to `var/home`, and /var is not the install-time sysroot.
#
# So the key must go somewhere that (a) exists during %post and (b) is carried
# into the deployment: that is /etc. sshd is told to read it with an
# AuthorizedKeysFile drop-in, which is what the %post below writes.
#
# VERIFIED, not assumed: on the RC1c VM, with the user's own
# ~/.ssh/authorized_keys renamed away so it could not mask the result, sshd
# authenticated using ONLY /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/test, with SELinux
# enforcing. `sshd -T` reported:
#       authorizedkeysfile .ssh/authorized_keys /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/%u
#
# NOTES ON THE KEY ITSELF
#   * The username must match the account created in Anaconda's User Creation
#     spoke. SP+ ships no human account (DN-13).
#     UNLIKE the old `sshkey` command, a WRONG USERNAME HERE FAILS QUIETLY -- the
#     file is simply never read by sshd, because it is named after the user. Check
#     it. The verification step at the bottom of this file is how.
#   * The key MUST be a public key. A line containing "PRIVATE KEY" is a mistake
#     serious enough that you should assume the key is compromised and rotate it.
#   * One key per line. Add more lines to the same file for more keys.
#
# WHY THE DROP-IN IS NUMBERED 46
# sshd takes the FIRST obtained value for each keyword, so an earlier-sorting
# drop-in wins. 45-sp-plus.conf is the image's hardening (PasswordAuthentication
# no, KbdInteractiveAuthentication no, PermitRootLogin no, PubkeyAuthentication
# yes). 46 sits immediately after it and before Fedora's 50-redhat.conf, so this
# setting cannot be lost to a future Fedora change, and it cannot pre-empt the
# hardening above it. A key placed here is the ONLY way in.
# =============================================================================

%post --erroronfail --interpreter=/bin/bash --log=/root/sp-plus-operator-key.log
set -euo pipefail

# REPLACE BOTH PLACEHOLDERS. Leaving them as-is is intended to fail loudly.
OPERATOR_USER="REPLACE_WITH_THE_USERNAME_CREATED_IN_ANACONDA"
OPERATOR_KEY="ssh-ed25519 REPLACE_WITH_THE_OPERATOR_PUBLIC_KEY operator@example.invalid"

# Fail the install rather than produce a machine you cannot reach, or one that
# trusts a placeholder. This is the loud failure the old `sshkey` line never gave.
case "$OPERATOR_USER" in REPLACE_WITH_*)
  echo "OPERATOR KEY OVERLAY: username placeholder was not replaced" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
case "$OPERATOR_KEY" in *REPLACE_WITH_*)
  echo "OPERATOR KEY OVERLAY: key placeholder was not replaced" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
case "$OPERATOR_KEY" in *"PRIVATE KEY"*)
  echo "OPERATOR KEY OVERLAY: that is a PRIVATE key. Rotate it and use the .pub" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
case "$OPERATOR_KEY" in ssh-ed25519\ *|ssh-rsa\ *|ecdsa-sha2-*|sk-ssh-*|sk-ecdsa-*) ;;
  *) echo "OPERATOR KEY OVERLAY: not a recognised public key line" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac

install -d -m 755 /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d
printf '%s\n' "$OPERATOR_KEY" > "/etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/${OPERATOR_USER}"
chmod 644 "/etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/${OPERATOR_USER}"

# sshd must be told to look there. .ssh/authorized_keys is kept FIRST so the
# ordinary per-user path keeps working exactly as it does on stock Fedora.
printf 'AuthorizedKeysFile .ssh/authorized_keys /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/%%u\n' \
  > /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/46-sp-plus-operator.conf
chmod 644 /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/46-sp-plus-operator.conf

# SELinux is enforcing; an unlabelled file here authenticates nobody.
restorecon -RF /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d 2>/dev/null || :

# THE TREE IS THE EVIDENCE, NOT THE EXIT CODE. Refuse to finish an install that
# produced an unreachable machine.
test -s "/etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/${OPERATOR_USER}"
grep -q 'authorized_keys.d' /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/46-sp-plus-operator.conf
echo "OPERATOR KEY OVERLAY: installed for ${OPERATOR_USER}"
%end

# VERIFY AFTER THE FIRST BOOT -- do not assume this worked:
#   ssh -i /path/to/the/private/key OPERATOR_USER@THE_MACHINE  # Replace both
# If it refuses, on the machine's own console run:
#   sudo sshd -T | grep -i authorizedkeysfile
#   sudo cat /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/OPERATOR_USER          # Replace
# and confirm the filename matches the account name exactly.
